# A fixed name for the gadget's serial port, ON THE HOST.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT INSTALLED BY ANY RECIPE AND IS NOT ON THE BOARD. It
# belongs to the machine the Pi is plugged into. Copy it there:
#
#     sudo install -m0644 70-pi-gadget.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#     sudo udevadm control --reload
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
#
# The ACM function appears on the host as /dev/ttyACM0, or ttyACM1, or
# whichever number is free when it enumerates. Anything that scripts
# against it breaks the day a second ACM device is plugged in, and the
# symptom is a terminal that opens successfully onto the wrong device.
#
# /dev/pi-console is stable because it is matched on the vendor and
# product ids of the gadget itself.
#
# 1d6b:0104 is the Linux Foundation's own vendor id and the multifunction
# composite gadget product id, which is what bench-gadget writes into
# idVendor and idProduct. If those are ever changed on the board, this
# rule has to change with them.

SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1d6b", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0104", \
    SYMLINK+="pi-console"

# The keyboard function is deliberately NOT given a symlink. It is a
# plain HID keyboard and the host should treat it as one: adding a rule
# would suggest there is something to configure, and there is not. That
# is the point of using boot protocol.
#
# The network function is handled by NetworkManager rather than udev;
# see nm-pi-gadget.nmconnection beside this file.
